<a id="vertical-slices"></a>
### Vertical slices

The language grows in thin vertical slices, each fully proven end to end before the next starts ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-vertical-slices-2026-11-06)).

**The slice shape** (the same `done when` for every slice): the slice's rules are Lean judgments
with accepted, refused and trapping conformance examples; its programs run from source through
every stage to BIR and both backends, each stage proved or checked by a proved validator; the
composed theorem covers the slice under named premises, LLVM and LCB premises separate; the facts
report and diagnostics cover its constructs; its examples pass on the Lean semantics and on both
backends, and CI compares their full observations.

**The order** (agreed unanimously by Claude, Codex, Kimi and agy; owner-approved 2026-11-06,
revised 2026-11-07). Later slices extend earlier constructs without reintroducing them. Not
scheduled: `free` (OD59), `trusted mod` (OD61), mutable global cells (OD8), `process_exit` (OD136),
process spawning (Q7.4), foreign function values (OD4). Concurrency is the final language slice;
its model remains to be decided when that slice comes.

| slice | adds | phase |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | `main -> u8`, immutable typed `u8` lets, decimal literals (leading zero refused), reads, parentheses, checked `+`, return; `concrete build` and `concrete facts`; the diagnostics record; both backends; reproducible builds; each program's maximum stack use, measured from the final binary on both backends, with the `Concrete.toml` stack budget; the trusted-base statement in facts; the random-program differential fuzzer; AddressSanitizer; binary-size tracking and the compile-speed budget; the frozen, versioned JSON schema for diagnostics and facts from the first front-end output | B0 |
| 1 | the approved component (row Q8.2): an empty non-`Copy` struct, its literal, a move, the use-after-move refusal, a struct-destructuring `let`, one checked operation, a return; explicit `abort()` as the runtime trap example and the refused `200 + 56` control; the component and bound launcher; the predictable policy; `Concrete.toml` `[policy]` and `[assumptions]`; the Lean I/O driver infrastructure | B1 |
| LP0 | BIR → LCB → native proof for slices 0 and 1 (R-0572); no syntax | LP0 |
| 2 | all integer widths, literal forms, unary `-`, `- * / %`, bitwise operations, shifts, lossless `as`, `wrapping_*`, `saturating_*`, the named lossy conversions (OD99); all values known | C |
| 3 | `bool`, comparisons (OD42), `&&`, `\|\|`, `!` (Q6.5), `Unit`, value `if`/`else`, trailing value expressions and the Unit statement rule (OD29), the head rule (OD16, OD34), `let mut`, assignment, no shadowing (OD67), `discard` (OD62); the octagon checker through branches and joins; `checked_no_implicit_traps`; the may-abort fact | C |
| 4 | `while`, `for i from a until b` (OD47–OD49), labels, `break`, `continue`, `#[invariant]`, `#[variant]`, `#[may_not_terminate("reason")]` on loops; the loop rule; termination facts; the step-counting cost model for loops | C |
| 5 | functions, parameters, calls, recursion with one function `#[variant]` or permission per recursive component (OD82), `#[requires]`, `#[ensures]`, literal expected types (OD31, OD138); closed call evidence; stack and depth facts from final artifacts; proven stack bounds for recursion; the step-counting cost model extended through calls | C |
| 6 | authority: `with(...)` over the reserved set (no spawning), the inclusion judgment (TRUST-4), the primitive effect table, `main` grant parameters, `give_up` and `close_dir`, launcher minting, startup refusal 125, the start and exit trace; capability and reachable-grant facts; the entropy capability granted to `main` | C |
