docs/decisions.md:179:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
docs/decisions.md:181:**The Core rows are a provisional approval (stage A2a).** They bind the design without freezing it. Stage A2b formalizes them in a proposed `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` and proves the four design theorems; any row the formalization changes returns to the owner; stage A2i integrates the parts (2026-11-06); the owner freezes Core at A2c (row Q9.19 as revised). Until a rule is written in Lean, the answer below governs over any chapter text that differs.
docs/decisions.md:183:<a id="decided-2026-11-05-revisions"></a>**Owner-approved revisions of recorded rules** (each also noted at the rule it revises):
docs/decisions.md:187:- **TRAIT-1@1** (row Q2.8): two impls with equal heads may coexist only when Copy/Destroy disjointness (OD28) makes their domains disjoint, as in `impl<T: Copy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>` beside `impl<T: Destroy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>`. No other coherence or orphan rule changes, and no disjunctive bound syntax is added.
docs/decisions.md:189:- **Authority rules 13, 5 and 7** (row Q7.11): `Console` joins the reserved capability set, for operations on the `StdIn`, `StdOut` and `StdErr` grants; rule 5's operational list and rule 7's primitive table include it. `Device` stays for device grants.
docs/decisions.md:191:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
docs/decisions.md:194:<a id="decided-2026-11-05-new-rules"></a>**New rules** (P4): duplicate variant names in one enum and duplicate method names in one impl are refused, like duplicate fields (bug 313); the rule gets its ID and diagnostic when it is written in Lean.
docs/decisions.md:215:- **Q2.5** What happens when drain processing fails? — On Stop, `Drained { at, survivor, rest, err }`; `map_into` returns `Stopped { done, survivor, rest, err }`.
docs/decisions.md:218:- **Q2.8** How is 'Copy or Destroy' written for container impls? — (a) separate impls for every admitted combination of Copy/Destroy payload bounds, no disjunctive-bound syntax: two each for Heap, HeapArray and Vec, three for Result, four for OrderedMap; coherence may use Copy/Destroy disjointness. *Revises or adds: TRAIT-1@1 (under OD6): equal generic impl heads allowed only when Copy/Destroy disjointness makes their domains disjoint; all other coherence and orphan rules unchanged. Option (b) would also add new bound syntax.*
docs/decisions.md:220:- **Q3.2** How are views passed and lent? — Downward parameters; mutable lending freezes origin.
docs/decisions.md:222:- **Q3.4** Which view accesses conflict? — Existing whole-root conflict rules.
docs/decisions.md:225:- **Q3.7** What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy? — Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): the address is read from a read-only target profile; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile is unchanged, and the provenance rules and the zero-byte exception are preserved.*
docs/decisions.md:237:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
docs/decisions.md:242:- **Q6.1** What determines mandatory static refusal? — Fixed Lean-defined local refusal rules. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): static refusal widens from proven violations to unproven safety.*
docs/decisions.md:243:- **Q6.2** Is local constant propagation mandatory? — Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`. *Revised 2026-11-05: facts propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring ([revision](#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields)).* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): known values remain facts; refusal widens to unproven safety.*
docs/decisions.md:252:- **Q7.4** How does process inheritance work? — Deferred: this target offers no process spawning.
docs/decisions.md:258:- **Q7.10** Do you approve the linux-x86_64-hosted grant table? — Approve R4 F6's complete grant table below as part of this row: every launch configuration, operation signature, permission, narrowing rule and ending contract. Standard-stream operations require Console (Q7.11), not Device; where the R4 F6 table says with(Device) for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, read with(Console). A missing configured grant refuses startup with exit 125; a trap exits 134. *Revises or adds: New target contract: owner decision.* *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.* *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q16): the grant table is a lawful extensible registry; the existing grant contracts are preserved, an unknown operation is refused, and the Entropy grant's shape waits for slice 6 ([question 14](#foundation-q14)).*
docs/decisions.md:259:- **Q7.11** Which permission do the standard streams use? — Add a Console capability for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr operations; keep Device for device grants. *Revises or adds: Authority rule 13 (adds Console); rules 5 and 7 lists include it.*
docs/decisions.md:261:- **Q8.2** What exact source subset is admitted? — A1 minimal `u8`/linear-struct component subset. <a id="q8-2-revision"></a>Confirmed 2026-11-06 by [vertical slices](#decided-project-vertical-slices-2026-11-06): this is slice 1; its defined trap is an explicit `abort()`, with the refused `200 + 56` as its known-violation control; integers are complete at slice 2.
docs/decisions.md:280:- **Q9.14** What must pass before Core freezes? — Provisional approval, then four kernel-checked theorems against a proposed Spec.lean, then freeze. Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): the integration stage A2i (interaction proofs, semantic contract, modular layout, owner view, outside-reader pass) also passes before the freeze.
docs/decisions.md:281:- **Q9.15** Where do normative definitions and documentation live? — One main normative Lean file with doc comments. *Revises or adds: Generated-chapter requirement (philosophy rule 6 / earlier roadmap commitment): replaced by one normative Lean file with doc comments and examples, a browsable rendering of that Lean, and coverage-gated retirement of prose chapters; your decision of 2026-11-04.*
docs/decisions.md:285:- **Q9.19** How do approval, formalization and Core freeze relate? — A2a → A2b → A2c; Spec.lean headed PROPOSED until freeze; R-0560 depends on the freeze. *Revises or adds: Process: owner decision.* Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): integration (A2i) comes between A2b and the A2c freeze, and the modular layout (R-0560) precedes the freeze.
docs/decisions.md:289:- **P1** Where agent discretion ends — Claude and Codex reason every question from the philosophy and recommend; they never pick a language answer through code, fixtures, diagnostics or docs. 'Important' = anything that revises a decided rule, changes observable language meaning you have not ruled on, or that you reserved. Every important decision comes to you.
docs/decisions.md:290:- **P2** Priority order when principles conflict — Soundness and provability > transparency (with auditability, honest trust boundaries and compiler-owned identity) > one authoritative definition (checked derived views allowed) > explicitness at the use site > brevity. The philosophy guides open choices and never silently reopens decided rules.
docs/decisions.md:292:- **P4** Duplicate enum variants or methods (bug 313) — Refuse duplicate variant names in one enum and duplicate method names in one impl, with a new rule and diagnostic, the same as duplicate fields.
docs/decisions.md:293:- **P5** legacy/ is a byte-frozen snapshot, never patched or run — legacy/ is main's tree at the move commit, byte-frozen. A root check compares its tree hash, allowing only deletions a satisfied RETENTION.tsv entry authorizes. No legacy gate ever runs. The oracle is the tag-built artifact (P13), not the legacy/ copy.
docs/decisions.md:294:- **P6** Retention manifest gates deleting legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv at the root gives every legacy asset (corpus, regressions, examples, std, bug ledger, site, docs, research) a destination and deletion criterion. Revised 2026-11-06 ([restart row](#decided-project-restart)): nothing is deleted at B3; legacy/, the old pipeline included, is deleted way later (LD: once the new compiler implements a full real program such as RocksDB, ethrex or similar), when RETENTION.tsv has no live entry, a final ideas sweep has dispositioned every idea and the owner explicitly approves.
docs/decisions.md:295:- **P7** Prove the new landing path before moving — T0: land beside the old tree the new root CI, required-checks.txt (enforced by train watch and land), RETENTION.tsv, the task disposition table, ORACLE.lock with the oracle artifact, and ISSUES.tsv. T1: rehearse the move on a throwaway branch: fresh clone, build, hook, exact-SHA CI, train --dry-run land; a broken build, a failing gate and a missing required check must each be refused. T2: the move in one train.
docs/decisions.md:300:- **P12** Issue record from day one — From day one, ISSUES.tsv at the root: id, date, area (spec|proof|compiler|launcher|ci|migration), evidence, owner, severity, closure condition, status, with a small root check. Spec, proof, launcher, CI and migration defects go there. The full bug ledger and tool return with slice 1.
docs/decisions.md:301:- **P13** concrete-ci-2 as the only CI host — CI runs on concrete-ci-2 (self-hosted) and on GitHub-hosted runners as a regular second pool, not only a fallback. Jobs that need ci-2 caches run there; the oracle is fetched as a release asset verified by digest; a check neither pool can run is incomplete and blocks landing. Self-hosted runners never run pull_request jobs.
docs/decisions.md:304:- **P16** Status changes in the refusal list — Accept the new statuses in PHILOSOPHY.md. Now Permanent: operator overloading (OD26), capability hiding (authority rule 5), derived equality, mutable global state (OD8), umbrella capabilities and aliases, old/quantifiers in contracts (OD117), lexical shadowing (OD67), and ghost code that affects execution. Ghost code itself is admitted as decided (OD81). Foreign function values reopen when typed extern fn types are admitted (OD4); concurrency reopens on R-0399's trigger.
docs/decisions.md:312:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
