docs/decisions.md:1254:**Only the project owner decides these items, OD1 – OD157.** All are answered. OD1 – OD32 (exit 2n, closed) and OD47 were decided on 2026-10-29 (OD23 – OD32 by the strictness pass of that day). OD33 – OD157 were decided on 2026-10-31, by the owner's rule that an answer every reviewer agreed on and as strict as Austral or stricter is taken, and on 2026-11-01, when the owner approved the final set for the rest; the authority decision of the same day supersedes OD38's and OD156's `Root` readings (OD94 was withdrawn: MOD-1 already refuses its case, bug 337). The answers are recorded in [docs/decisions.md](decisions.md), state the decided rules in chapters now (owner revision, 2026-11-03), then become Lean in phases B–D, and are enforced as each new-compiler slice lands. The questions raised while recording them were answered on 2026-11-02, the owner deciding the first four and, by the owner's rule of that day, Claude and Codex agreeing on the best long-term answer for the rest ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02)); they revise OD24, OD38, OD43, OD83, OD102, OD146 and OD156 and the authority rules. The spec audit's questions were answered the same day ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)), revising OD140 and STMT-13. Traits are exported and imported like any other declaration (owner, 2026-11-02). Core was provisionally approved on 2026-11-05 ([record](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); A2b formalization is current, and the A2c freeze awaits the four design theorems, the A2i integration stage (2026-11-06) and an outside-reader pass. New questions follow the same rule: No agent, orchestrator or
docs/decisions.md:1305:| OD15 | <a id="od15"></a>G10: whole-owner freezing under a shared borrow stays. G19: no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity, and the operational loan rules are defined now | T-G10 (keep the freeze) and T-G19 (the policy sentence) | exit 2i (the G10 decision record), so every deliverable; R-0496, R-0505 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner. (a) Whole-owner freezing: a live borrow of any part of a value freezes the whole owner, and loans are keyed by the root binding (REF-11 as in the current compiler); this tightens OD2's part 2: at a call, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, even disjoint fields (`h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused, as in the current compiler); OD1's "keyed by the root variable" stands. (b) The strict option: the language guarantees `&mut` exclusivity, which the checker enforces (OD2, OD15a), and no compiler pass or optimization may assume or exploit it until the checker's guarantee is proven in Lean (R-0499 with the Core semantics R-0496); until then the operational loan rules are defined now, G19 included: a pointer or alias derived from a loan is valid only while that loan lives. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319, which OD23 adds) and REF-14 (pending, R-0496) **Canonical current root-based exclusivity subject: REF-11 (including OD23’s re-lent case); OD2 and OD23 refer here rather than defining separate variants.** |
docs/decisions.md:1391:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
docs/decisions.md:1409:| OD119 | <a id="od119"></a>Buffered output when the process aborts (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether output the program wrote before a trap or abort is flushed before the process ends. Bears on STMT-1, STMT-2, PASS-13, OPEN-101. The current compiler: print builtins write through C's buffered stdio, and `abort` does not flush it on glibc (a binary that prints `hello` and then traps prints nothing on Linux) while it does on Darwin; the interpreter models the same buffer and the same platform difference. STMT-2 says a failure ends the process at once and is silent on output already written, so the observable trace of a trapping program differs by platform. Recorded by [INTERPRETER_TRUST.md](../legacy/docs/verification/INTERPRETER_TRUST.md) §2. Options: (a) everything written before the trap is output (flush on abort, or unbuffered output); (b) what was written is unspecified on a trap, and PASS-13 compares output only for exiting runs. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-119 | see OPEN-119 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Everything written before the trap is output |
docs/decisions.md:1410:| OD120 | <a id="od120"></a>A vacuous contract (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a contract whose precondition is unsatisfiable is refused by the language or only reported, under a project policy. Bears on CON-1, CON-2. The current compiler: `#[requires(x > 5 && x < 3)]` compiles; a decided-unsatisfiable precondition makes the claim `vacuous`, never `proved`, and E0613 refuses it only under the `forbid-vacuous` policy (`Concrete.Policy`), not in a plain build. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) states the reporting rule. Options: (a) a language refusal in every build; (b) tooling only: always reported `vacuous`, refused under a policy, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-120 | see OPEN-120 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Tooling: reported `vacuous`, refused under policy **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a vacuous precondition used for safety admission is refused in every build.** |
docs/decisions.md:1414:| OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../legacy/docs/platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
docs/decisions.md:1418:| OD128 | <a id="od128"></a>`->` between a match arm's pattern and body (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a match arm may separate its pattern from its body with `->` as well as `=>`. Bears on SYN-1 and PAT-7. The current compiler: the parser accepts both (`parseMatchArms`; `2 -> { return 42; }` exits 42 on both consumers), and the grammar's `match_arm` has only `=>`. The syntax freeze review (2026-04, [SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW.md) §1.5) recommended `=>` as the canonical form with a warning for `->`, which also spells a return type. Options: (a) `=>` only, `->` refused; (b) both accepted; (c) both accepted, with a warning on `->`, as the freeze review recommended. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-128 | see OPEN-128 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `=>` only |
docs/decisions.md:1419:| OD129 | <a id="od129"></a>Type arguments of an enum literal taken from its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether an enum literal or variant written without type arguments (`Option::Some { value: 42 }`, `Option::None`) takes them from the type its position expects, and from which positions. Bears on TYPE-7, TYPE-15 and OPEN-46, which asks the same of a generic call's type arguments and whose pick excludes the expected result type. The current compiler: the type arguments come from the expected type of a `return` (the function's result type), of an annotated `let` and of a call argument (the parameter's type); all three exit 42 on both consumers, and spec examples rely on it (PAT-1). The syntax freeze review (§1.3, kill-list items 1, 4 and 5) proposed this inference for enum literals and for constructor calls such as `Vec::new()`. Options: (a) explicit type arguments always; (b) from the expected type in the positions the current compiler uses; (c) the answer to OPEN-46, applied to enum literals. Austral: type parameters are inferred from the arguments, and a declaration's type can disambiguate. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-129 | see OPEN-129 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Same rule as OD46: type arguments from the arguments, otherwise explicit (`None::<i32>`) |
docs/decisions.md:1426:| OD136 | <a id="od136"></a>std's `process_exit` (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/compiler and docs/platform). Question: Whether std keeps `process_exit`, which ends the process through libc `exit` without running any deferred call or consuming the caller's owned values. Bears on STMT-7, OWN-2 and TRUST-4. The current compiler: `std.process.process_exit(code: i32) with(Process)` exists and calls libc `exit`; the language itself has no exit operation (STMT-7). The design record [MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md) calls an exit operation deliberately absent, since in a linear language it would bypass the proof that every owned value is consumed. Options: (a) keep it, stating that it skips cleanup and that `Process` is what grants it; (b) remove it, status flowing back through `main`'s return only. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/platform, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-136 | see OPEN-136 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
