128:These items were undesigned when the chapters were cleaned up on 2026-11-03. **The owner approved their design provisionally on 2026-11-05** ([the decided rows](#decided-2026-11-05-core): allocator identities Q1, allocation contracts Q1–Q2, the heap-array view Q3, static text Q4, capability inference Q5, unsafe contracts Q3.7–Q3.12, static refusal Q6, grants Q7, semantic objects Q8–Q9). Where a chapter still says **undecided: see the target Core proposal**, read the decided row; the rule is rewritten in Lean at stage A2b and frozen at A2c. The proposal is owned by
181:**The Core rows are a provisional approval (stage A2a).** They bind the design without freezing it. Stage A2b formalizes them in a proposed `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` and proves the four design theorems; any row the formalization changes returns to the owner; stage A2i integrates the parts (2026-11-06); the owner freezes Core at A2c (row Q9.19 as revised). Until a rule is written in Lean, the answer below governs over any chapter text that differs.
220:- **Q3.2** How are views passed and lent? — Downward parameters; mutable lending freezes origin.
280:- **Q9.14** What must pass before Core freezes? — Provisional approval, then four kernel-checked theorems against a proposed Spec.lean, then freeze. Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): the integration stage A2i (interaction proofs, semantic contract, modular layout, owner view, outside-reader pass) also passes before the freeze.
285:- **Q9.19** How do approval, formalization and Core freeze relate? — A2a → A2b → A2c; Spec.lean headed PROPOSED until freeze; R-0560 depends on the freeze. *Revises or adds: Process: owner decision.* Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): integration (A2i) comes between A2b and the A2c freeze, and the modular layout (R-0560) precedes the freeze.
293:- **P5** legacy/ is a byte-frozen snapshot, never patched or run — legacy/ is main's tree at the move commit, byte-frozen. A root check compares its tree hash, allowing only deletions a satisfied RETENTION.tsv entry authorizes. No legacy gate ever runs. The oracle is the tag-built artifact (P13), not the legacy/ copy.
297:- **P9** Website during the transition — The website goes down at the move and is redone from scratch with the new compiler; no frozen playground is served. Unpublishing the live site is an outward action whose moment the owner confirms.
298:- **P10** What 'closed' means for old-compiler bugs — Old-compiler defects become 'retired with the old implementation', never 'fixed'. Each keeps its reproducer, ruling and structural class; 106 reproducers feed the oracle comparisons. Bugs the frozen browser compiler still shows stay visible while the site serves it.
310:- **P21** Retention records outlive legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv, ISSUES.tsv, required-checks.txt, scripts/oracle/ and docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/ (with task_disposition.tsv) are part of the day-one root, outside legacy/, so they survive deleting it. *Revised 2026-11-06 (owner, verbatim: "do the whole movement"): of docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/, the files that are decision sources or disposition records stay at the root (task_disposition.tsv, the decision log ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md, the task register ROADMAP_TASKS.md, ROADMAP_RECONCILIATION.md, ROADMAP_RESTRUCTURE.md with its table, and the archive README); the old control plane ROADMAP.md, ROADMAP_BACKLOG.md, ROADMAP_DIRECTION.md and WORK_QUEUE.md were duplicates of their frozen legacy/ copies apart from the move's mechanical rewrites, and nothing cites them as a decision source, so their root copies were removed and links point at the legacy/ copies, which stay until the legacy deletion point. The other docs/archive/ records (the site takedown record, the old bug ledger's disposition, the landing audit and the dated decision archives) stay at the root.*
312:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
